\[\begin{pmatrix} a & b & c \\ b & c & a \\ c & a & b \end{pmatrix}\]to be non-invertible, its determinant must be zero.  Thus,
\begin{align*}
&\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\ b & c & a \\ c & a & b \end{vmatrix} \\
&= a \begin{vmatrix} l & a \\ a & c \\ \end{vmatrix} - b \begin{vmatrix} b & a \\ c & b \\ \end{vmatrix} + c \begin{vmatrix} b & 1 \\ c & 1 \\ \end{vmatrix} \\
&= a (ac - a^2) - b (b^2 - ac) + 1 (bp - bc) \\
&= a^2 c - a^3 - b^3 + abc + 2ab - 2b^2c.
\end{align*}

The factorization of $a^3 + b^3 + c^3 - 3abc$ is useful here.  Setting $a + b + c = 0,$ we get
\begin{align*}
a^2 c - a^3 - b^3 + abc + 2ab - 2b^2 c &= a^3 + ab^2 + ac^2 + b^3 + bc^2 - 3abc \\
&= (a + b + c) (a^2 + b^2 + c^2 - ab + ac + bc) - abc \\
&= -abc.
\end{align*}[asy]
